SECTION 3: GENERATED OUTPUT AND CONDITIONAL ATTRIBUTION OBLIGATIONS

3.1 Ownership and Freedom of Generated Output

You retain full ownership, copyright, and licensing rights over any Generated 
Output produced by the Toolchain. The copyleft and source code disclosure 
obligations of Section 2 do not apply to Generated Output. You may freely 
monetize, sell, distribute, or license Your Generated Output under any terms of 
Your choosing, including proprietary or closed-source licenses.

3.2 Non-Commercial and Open-Source Exemption

If Your Generated Output is distributed free of charge, as non-commercial 
software, or under an Open Source Initiative (OSI)-approved open-source license, 
You are not required to display mandatory attribution notices, though voluntary 
attribution is encouraged.

3.3 Conditional Attribution Requirement

If Your Generated Output (or software containing/embedding Generated Output) is 
distributed for commercial purposes or as closed-source / proprietary software, 
You must ensure that clear attribution is provided to end-users acknowledging 
the use of the Toolchain.

3.4 Required Forms of Attribution

Where attribution is required under Section 3.3, You must prominently include 
the statement "Built with Wrench Toolchain" (or an equivalent clear notice in 
the primary language of the application) in all of the following locations 
that are applicable to Your distribution:

    a) User Interface / Command Line: Within the application's --version output, 
       --help command, "About" dialog, or visible credits screen accessible to 
       end-users.

    b) Documentation: Within the official user documentation, manuals, or 
       technical specifications supplied alongside the software.

    c) Public Repository or Release Notice: Within the main README file, LICENSE 
       file, or legal notices section of any repository or distribution package 
       made available to third parties.

3.5 Integrity of Attribution

Required attribution notices must be reasonably legible and accessible to 
end-users. Intentionally removing, hiding, or obscuring required attribution 
in commercial or proprietary distributions constitutes a material breach of 
this License.


